        "## 1.1 Definition\n",
        "\n",
        "Multicollinearity occurs when two or more predictor variables $X_i$ and $X_j$ in a regression model are highly linearly correlated. Formally, for a regression:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\cdots + \\beta_k X_k + \\varepsilon$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Perfect multicollinearity exists when:\n",
        "$$X_i = \\alpha_0 + \\alpha_1 X_j \\quad \\text{for some constants } \\alpha_0, \\alpha_1$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This causes the matrix $\\mathbf{X}^\\top\\mathbf{X}$ to become singular (non-invertible), making the OLS estimator $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}} = (\\mathbf{X}^\\top\\mathbf{X})^{-1}\\mathbf{X}^\\top\\mathbf{Y}$ undefined or highly unstable.\n",
        "\n",
        "The degree of multicollinearity is commonly measured using the **Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)**:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{VIF}_j = \\frac{1}{1 - R_j^2}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $R_j^2$ is the coefficient of determination from regressing $X_j$ on all other predictors. A VIF > 10 is a standard threshold for problematic multicollinearity.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.2 Description\n",
        "\n",
        "Multicollinearity arises when predictor variables in a regression model contain redundant information, making it impossible for the model to isolate the unique contribution of each variable. In financial modeling, this is extremely common because assets in the same sector — such as tech stocks or bank stocks — tend to move together in response to the same macroeconomic forces."
      ]
