        "## 2.1 Definition\n",
        "\n",
        "Overfitting occurs when a model learns the noise in training data rather than the underlying signal, resulting in excellent in-sample performance but poor out-of-sample generalization.\n",
        "\n",
        "Formally, for a model with parameters $\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ trained on data $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{train}}$, overfitting is present when:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathcal{L}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}; \\mathcal{D}_{\\text{train}}) \\ll \\mathcal{L}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}; \\mathcal{D}_{\\text{test}})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\mathcal{L}$ is the loss function (e.g., mean squared error). In polynomial regression of degree $p$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + \\beta_2 X^2 + \\cdots + \\beta_p X^p + \\varepsilon$$\n",
        "\n",
        "as $p \\to n$ (number of observations), the in-sample $R^2 \\to 1$ while the out-of-sample MSE explodes. The bias-variance tradeoff formalizes this:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{Expected MSE} = \\text{Bias}^2 + \\text{Variance} + \\sigma^2_{\\varepsilon}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Overfitting corresponds to low bias but excessive variance.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 2.2 Description\n",
        "\n",
        "Overfitting happens when a model is so complex that it fits the specific training data perfectly — including the random noise — but fails completely when applied to new, unseen data. In volatility modeling, an overfit model may appear to explain past market behavior brilliantly while producing dangerous mispricings on live trades."
      ]
