        "## 3.1 Definition\n",
        "\n",
        "Factor prioritization refers to the problem of identifying which predictors $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k$ in a model contribute most meaningfully to explaining the target variable $Y$, especially when the number of candidate factors $k$ is large relative to the sample size $n$.\n",
        "\n",
        "One formal framework is the **Lasso** (Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator), which solves:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^{\\text{Lasso}} = \\arg\\min_{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}} \\left\\{ \\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\mathbf{x}_i^\\top \\boldsymbol{\\beta})^2 + \\lambda \\sum_{j=1}^k |\\beta_j| \\right\\}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The L1 penalty $\\lambda \\sum |\\beta_j|$ shrinks irrelevant coefficients exactly to zero, performing automatic feature selection. The **importance score** of feature $j$ can also be quantified via standardized coefficients or permutation importance:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{Importance}_j = \\frac{|\\hat{\\beta}_j| \\cdot \\text{SD}(X_j)}{\\text{SD}(Y)}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## 3.2 Description\n",
        "\n",
        "Prioritizing factors is the challenge of determining which variables in a large set of potential predictors are actually driving a financial outcome, rather than simply adding noise. In multi-asset volatility models, a desk may have dozens of candidate risk factors — sector returns, macro variables, sentiment indices — and must identify the small subset that genuinely matters before building a tradeable model."
      ]
