        "## 4.1 Definition\n",
        "\n",
        "A model suffers from lack of interpretation when its internal structure does not permit practitioners to explain *why* it produces a given output in terms of economically meaningful variables. This is formalized through the concept of **model explainability**, measured using tools such as SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations):\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\hat{y}_i = \\phi_0 + \\sum_{j=1}^k \\phi_{ij}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\phi_{ij}$ is the SHAP value representing the marginal contribution of feature $j$ to observation $i$'s prediction. For a linear model, interpretation is transparent:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\hat{Y} = \\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\hat{\\beta}_1 X_1 + \\cdots + \\hat{\\beta}_k X_k$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Each coefficient $\\hat{\\beta}_j$ has a direct interpretation: a one-unit change in $X_j$ changes $\\hat{Y}$ by $\\hat{\\beta}_j$, holding all else constant. Complex models (neural networks, random forests, kernel SVMs) lose this property, creating a **black box**.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4.2 Description\n",
        "\n",
        "Lack of interpretation arises when a model's predictions cannot be traced back to specific, economically meaningful inputs in a transparent way. On a derivatives desk this is not merely a philosophical issue — regulators, risk committees, and traders all require the ability to explain why a model is pricing a derivative at a particular level, and models that cannot provide this explanation create legal, regulatory, and operational risk."
      ]
