        "## 4.5 Diagnosis\n",
        "\n",
        "To recognize a lack of interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Coefficient Absence**: If a model does not produce a coefficient per input variable with a clear unit interpretation, it is not directly interpretable (e.g., a Random Forest produces only relative importance scores, not economic magnitudes).\n",
        "2. **Inability to Answer 'Why'**: Ask: \"If this option is priced at \\$5.20, which input variable is most responsible?\" If the model cannot answer this with a number and direction, interpretation is lacking.\n",
        "3. **Regulatory Review Failure**: Basel III / SR 11-7 requires banks to validate and explain model outputs. Models that fail internal model review due to unexplainability have a diagnosis confirmed by the regulator.\n",
        "4. **Sensitivity Testing Ambiguity**: If shocking a single input by 1% does not produce a clear, predictable, signed change in output, interpretation is compromised.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4.6 Damage\n",
        "\n",
        "Using uninterpretable models on a derivatives desk creates:\n",
        "- **Regulatory failure**: SR 11-7 (Federal Reserve model risk guidance) requires explainability of all risk models. Black-box models fail this standard and expose the firm to regulatory sanctions\n",
        "- **Trader distrust**: Traders will not hedge based on a model they cannot understand; untrustworthy models get overridden manually, negating the value of quantitative analysis entirely\n",
        "- **Inability to stress-test**: Risk managers cannot run meaningful scenario analysis (e.g., \"what happens to our vol surface if rates spike 100bps?\") without interpretable linkages between inputs and outputs\n",
        "- **P&L attribution failure**: Front-office reporting requires decomposing P&L into Greek contributions; black-box models cannot provide this decomposition\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4.7 Directions"
      ]
