        "# Step 4: Additional Challenge — Identifying a Solved Problem\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "Among the four challenges addressed in this handbook, **multicollinearity** is the challenge that has been most thoroughly solved in practice, with well-established, widely-adopted solutions backed by decades of econometric research.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why Multicollinearity Is Considered Solved\n",
        "\n",
        "Hoerl and Kennard (1970) introduced Ridge Regression specifically to address ill-conditioned $\\mathbf{X}^\\top\\mathbf{X}$ matrices caused by multicollinearity. By adding the L2 penalty $\\lambda\\|\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|^2$, the estimator becomes:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^{\\text{Ridge}} = (\\mathbf{X}^\\top\\mathbf{X} + \\lambda\\mathbf{I})^{-1}\\mathbf{X}^\\top\\mathbf{Y}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The addition of $\\lambda\\mathbf{I}$ guarantees that the matrix is invertible for any $\\lambda > 0$, directly eliminating the numerical instability caused by collinear predictors. This solution has three key properties established by Hoerl and Kennard:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. The Ridge estimator is **biased** but has **lower MSE** than OLS under collinearity\n",
        "2. There **always exists** a $\\lambda > 0$ such that $\\text{MSE}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^{\\text{Ridge}}) < \\text{MSE}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^{\\text{OLS}})$\n",
        "3. Modern cross-validation (as demonstrated in Challenge 1 above) provides a principled, data-driven method to select the optimal $\\lambda$\n",
        "\n",
        "Subsequent work by Tibshirani (1996) extended this to Lasso regression, which additionally performs variable selection — eliminating redundant predictors entirely rather than merely shrinking their coefficients. As demonstrated in Challenge 3, Lasso successfully reduced our 15-factor candidate set to the most relevant predictors while delivering comparable or better out-of-sample performance to the full OLS model.\n",
        "\n",
        "Both methods are now standard tools in financial econometrics, implemented in every major statistical computing environment, and routinely applied in portfolio risk models, factor attribution, and volatility surface calibration.\n",
        "\n",
